{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'ls -la /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_00-04-05-etcd_raft-real-run/draft/ && echo \"---\" && cat /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_00-04-05-etcd_raft-real-run/draft/update.json 2>/dev/null | head -50'","aggregated_output":"total 52\ndrwxrwxr-x 7 nitro nitro  4096 Oct 15 00:59 .\ndrwx------ 9 nitro nitro   260 Oct 15 00:59 ..\ndr-xr-xr-x 2 nitro nitro    40 Oct 15 00:59 .agents\ndr-xr-xr-x 2 nitro nitro    40 Oct 15 00:59 .aws\ndr-xr-xr-x 2 nitro nitro    40 Oct 15 00:59 .codex\ndr-xr-xr-x 2 nitro nitro    40 Oct 15 00:59 .git\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 nitro nitro 39799 Oct 15 00:56 assurance_generated_test.go\ndrwxrwxr-x 5 nitro nitro  4096 Oct 15 00:56 tmp\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 nitro nitro  4079 Oct 15 00:58 update.json\n---\n{\n \"rationale\": \"The boundary judgment I left unresolved has a source answer, so I read the contract context instead of parking it: the snapshot's Step documentation on both interfaces says only that it advances the state machine using the given message, the proto comments define the term field per message type but never assign term validation to the module, and doc.go documents that one network message (MsgProp) is deliberately term-less because send overwrites its term. That supports the reading that an unset term is a semantic marker for local or forwarded messages rather than an error signal the module must reject. I therefore refute the defect claim for the term-less network path while keeping the observation: the predicate is still contradicted, but no duty in the snapshot makes it a defect, and the byzantine-sender requirement is outside the crash-fault model the audit prioritises.\",\n \"sources\": [\n  {\n   \"id\": \"src_step_contract\",\n   \"file\": \"node.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 155,\n   \"end_line\": 156,\n   \"kind\": \"document_statement\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"id\": \"src_msgprop_doc\",\n   \"file\": \"doc.go\",\n   \"start_line\": 306,\n   \"end_line\": 312,\n   \"kind\": \"document_statement\"\n  }\n ],\n \"interpretations\": [\n  {\n   \"investigation_id\": \"0c740df08fd849a6a4c0743eb2ab947a-question-0\",\n   \"experiment_id\": \"0c740df08fd849a6a4c0743eb2ab947a-experiment\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"src_step_local\",\n    \"src_stepfollower_leadset\",\n    \"src_islocal\",\n    \"src_rawstep\",\n    \"src_step_contract\",\n    \"src_msgprop_doc\"\n   ],\n   \"executions\": {\n    \"2bb2e787750a4a799620f0b0d1673692\": [\n     56,\n     57\n    ]\n   },\n   \"conclusion\": \"violated\",\n   \"defect_claim\": \"refuted\",\n   \"rationale\": \"The observation stands and the judgment is now settled against a defect. Control and boundary both resolved: the leader's real heartbeat set follower 2's reported leader to 1 (index 56), and the same type from node 3 with no term field was accepted with no error and moved it to 3 (index 57), so the predicate that a message carrying no term must not change the leader context is contradicted. The defect claim is refuted on three sourced grounds, checked in the contract context rather than assumed: Step is documented only as advancing the state machine with a given message, on both the Node and RawNode interfaces, with no duty to validate the term field; doc.go documents that MsgProp is deliberately term-less because the send path overwrites its term, which shows an unset term is a marker for local or forwarded traffic rather than an error signal; and the path requires a sender that emits a network type with the field unset, which no correct peer does, so no crash-fault schedule reaches it and the module assumes peer correctness as Raft does. Consequences and limits are unchanged: a follower that adopts a peer on this basis misdirects its forwarded proposals and resets its election timer, the log self-heals on the next genuine leader message because that message carries a higher term and truncates the divergent tail, and nothing here alters the main finding, whose message needs no illegal field at all and whose duty the module does state through its own inbound filter.\",\n   \"counterevidence\": [\n    \"The strongest argument the other way is that the module accepts a message whose sender is not a member and whose term is unset, both of which a hardened implementation could check; the snapshot's contract does not require either check, which is why this is recorded as a boundary rather than a second finding.\",\n    \"The proposal-misdirection consequence is argued from the branch that sets lead, not observed in a receipt.\"\n   ],\n   \"limitations\": [\n    \"Single process with hand-delivered messages and one configuration; no receipt covers the misdirection consequence.\",\n    \"Refutation rests on the absence of a stated duty plus the documented term-less MsgProp; a future source statement assigning term validation to the module would reopen it.\",\n    \"How a transport should treat term-less messages is outside this module and outside the captured snapshot.\"\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
